 "observations": [
  "The page number «169» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the right the number of the table, «Tab. 58», in bold sans serif, underlined. At the foot, the signature line of the printed sheet: «D. K. D. Vidensk. Selsk. Skr., 7. Række, hist. og filos. Afd. III. 1.» on the left and the sheet number «22» on the right; recorded here, not transcribed as a line. The titles (L01-L02), in bold: O's «Tabula elgeib (O)», the siglum in roman, and the editor's title in square brackets, «[Tabula sinus]». The folios of the manuscripts stand at the right of the titles in two lines, «C 71v, O 129r—129v,» and «N 37v—38r.», the letters raised; here as right margin notes. Specks in the margins; not transcribed.",
  "The table: double vertical rules divide it into two halves, each of eight columns of arguments and three of the sine; double horizontal rules above and below the heads. The heads: «Semitae numerorum (CO) Lineae numerorum (N)» over the arguments of the left half and «Semitae numerornm (CO) Lineae numeri (N)» over those of the right, «Elgeib (CO Sinus (N)» and «Elgeib (CO) Sinus (N)» over the sines; the units «Sig.» and «Gr.» four times in each half and «Part.1)», «Min.2)», «Sec.3)», in smaller bold type. Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L03-L05), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. In the right head a small speck between the O of «(CO)» and the parenthesis; not transcribed.",
  "L06-L35, the arguments. Each row gives the four arcs that have the same sine, x, 180° − x, 180° + x and 360° − x, in signs and degrees (Suter, p. 69, JP2 0101, L24-L30): in the left half 0s x, 5s (30 − x), 6s x and 11s (30 − x) for x = 1° to 30°, in the right half 1s x, 4s (30 − x), 7s x and 10s (30 − x) for x = 31° to 60°, the last row 1s 0°, 5s 0°, 7s 0°, 11s 0° and 2s 0°, 4s 0°, 8s 0°, 10s 0°. The sines of 61° to 90° follow on p. 170.",
  "L06-L35, the sines. Checked: each value is 60 · sin x to within 1.2″ for x = 1° to 60°: within 0.9″ in every row except at 26° (18° 18′ 7″ for 18° 18′ 8.2″) and 33° (32° 40′ 41″ for 32° 40′ 42.0″), and 30° 0′ 0″ at 30°. The edition's readings in notes b-h, where C and O agree on a different figure, are those of the computation within 0.8″: 14° 30′ 55″, 28° 10′ 6″, 33° 33′ 6″, 34° 24′ 53″, 41° 40′ 46″, 45° 16′ 58″ and 49° 44′ 32″; at 8° (note a), where O has 50 and C 1, the edition prints 8° 21′ 2″, and the computation gives 8° 21′ 1.4″.",
  "L06-L35, compared with al-Battānī's sine table (Nallino, II, 55-56): the two tables agree at every whole degree from 1° to 60° except at 3°, 7°, 9°, 11°, 16°, 17°, 19°, 26°, 33°, 39°, 46°, 47°, 51°, 58° and 60°, where al-Khwārizmī's value is 1″ less, 3° 8′ 24″ against 3° 8′ 25″ and so on; in these fifteen places the computation lies between the two values, nearer to al-Battānī's in nine and to al-Khwārizmī's at 17°, 39°, 46°, 47°, 58° and 60°. Suter took al-Battānī's value where the manuscripts' reading differed from it by more than 1″ (p. 69, JP2 0101, L40-L44), so that all the edition's readings of notes a-h are al-Battānī's values; Suter and Björnbo explain the differences of 1″ by the halving of Ptolemy's chords with odd seconds rounded up or down (p. 70, JP2 0102, L01-L05).",
  "Notes 1-3: the units of the manuscripts: «Partes» in C and O and «Gradus» in N for the parts of the radius, «Dak.» (dakaicae) in C and O and «Min.» in N, «Elth.» or «Elthen.» (eltheniae, the seconds; Suter, p. 69, JP2 0101, L31-L32) in C and O and «Sec.» in N. Italics: «Partes» and «sive». L03, «Elgeib (CO» without its closing parenthesis, and «numerornm»: see the critical notes."
 ],
